        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "@daniel-samson can you share the code for Linux system trash? Within a minute of testing out this plugin I knew system trash needs to be supported. The trash directory of any good file manager has only ever meant system trash and I would rather not have to remember and manage files from trash differently in some weird editor plugin-specific directory. From the user standpoint, nothing is simpler than using a file manager that support systems trash like any decent ones or plugins for that matter. I often wish Mini's philosophy of keeping things simple to also mean it is simple for the users because--as in this example (and with mini.pick), artificial limitations impose a layer of complexity for no good reason.\n\nTrash files from mini.files are no different than any other trash files on the system. I don't see how optional dependencies are an issue and nothing would change for users who don't use the feature as the default is all they need. At the end of the day a good plugin is one that does its job well and works as expected without weird quirks, not because it has fewer lines of code for personal amusement.\n",
