            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I often wish Mini's philosophy of keeping things simple to also mean it is simple for the users because--as in this example (and with mini.pick), artificial limitations impose a layer of complexity for no good reason.\n\nMaking plugin easy to use *is* the goal of any 'mini.nvim' module. But unfortunately, it is the opposite of achieving an easy future-proof maintenance. And the number one design of this plugin is to balance both of them.\n\n> I don't see how optional dependencies are an issue and nothing would change for users who don't use the feature as the default is all they need. At the end of the day a good plugin is one that does its job well and works as expected without weird quirks, not because it has fewer lines of code for personal amusement.\n\nIt doesn't look like any more justification beyond what is presented here or in the documentation will be useful here. If you personally are not happy with how the plugin is designed or implemented, there is no need to add this extra information to the issue tracker. It doesn't really help 'mini.nvim' development.",
